#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10004
msgid "Monotonic Smooth Interpolation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10006
msgid ""
"The goal of monotonic smooth interpolation is to construct an "
"interpolation function that is globally monotonic from a given set of "
"data points $(x, y)$. This process therefore has two steps: smoothing and"
" interpolation. The smoothing step makes the data monotonic, and the "
"interpolation step then applies a monotonicity-preserving interpolator."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10009
msgid "Monotonic Interpolation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10011
msgid ""
"Given a set of data points $(x, y)$, `Gal3D` uses the [PCHIP "
"interpolator](https://doi.org/10.1137/0905021). This method not only "
"provides high-quality interpolation, but also preserves monotonicity and "
"local shape features."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10013
msgid ""
"Assume the data points satisfy $x_1 < x_2 < \\cdots < x_n$ with $n \\geq "
"2$, and the corresponding values are $y_i = f(x_i)$ for $i = 1, \\dots, "
"n$."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10015
msgid "We seek an interpolation function of the form"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10017
msgid ""
"\n"
"f_i(x) = a_i(x - x_i)^3 + b_i(x - x_i)^2 + c_i(x - x_i) + d_i\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10021
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Define $\\Delta x_i = x_{i+1} - x_i$, $\\Delta y_i = y_{i+1} - y_i$, and "
"the slope $\\Delta_i = \\Delta y_i / \\Delta x_i$."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10027
msgid "**Determining the derivatives at each node**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10029
msgid "For the endpoints $x_1$ and $x_n$:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10031
msgid ""
"If there are only two nodes, $(n = 2)$, the derivatives are set directly "
"equal to the slope: $f_1' = \\Delta_1$ and $f_2' = \\Delta_1$."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10034
msgid "If the number of nodes is $n > 2$, the Fritsch-Butland method is used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10036
msgid "For the left endpoint, the predicted derivative is"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10038
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"\n"
"G_1 = \\frac{(2\\Delta x_1 + \\Delta x_2)\\Delta_1 - \\Delta x_1 "
"\\Delta_2}{\\Delta x_1 + \\Delta x_2},\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10042
#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10059
#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10083
msgid "and the actual derivative is"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10044
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"\n"
"f_1' =\n"
"\\begin{cases}\n"
"0, & G_1 \\cdot \\Delta_1 \\leq 0, \\\\\n"
"3\\Delta_1, & \\Delta_1 \\cdot \\Delta_2 < 0 \\text{ and } |G_1| > "
"3|\\Delta_1|, \\\\\n"
"G_1, & \\text{otherwise.}\n"
"\\end{cases}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10053
msgid "For the right endpoint, the predicted derivative is"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10055
msgid ""
"\n"
"G_n = \\frac{(2\\Delta x_{n-1} + \\Delta x_{n-2})\\Delta_{n-1} - \\Delta "
"x_{n-1}\\Delta_{n-2}}{\\Delta x_{n-1} + \\Delta x_{n-2}},\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10061
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"\n"
"f_n' =\n"
"\\begin{cases}\n"
"0, & G_n \\cdot \\Delta_{n-1} \\leq 0, \\\\\n"
"3\\Delta_{n-1}, & \\Delta_{n-1} \\cdot \\Delta_{n-2} < 0 \\text{ and } "
"|G_n| > 3|\\Delta_{n-1}|, \\\\\n"
"G_n, & \\text{otherwise.}\n"
"\\end{cases}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10070
msgid "For an interior node $x_k$, the predicted derivative is"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10072
msgid ""
"\n"
"G_k = \\Delta_k \\Delta_{k-1} \\frac{W_1 + W_2}{W_1 \\Delta_k + W_2 "
"\\Delta_{k-1}},\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10076
msgid "where"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10078
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"\n"
"W_1 = 2\\Delta x_k + \\Delta x_{k-1}, \\qquad\n"
"W_2 = \\Delta x_k + 2\\Delta x_{k-1},\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10085
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"\n"
"f_k' =\n"
"\\begin{cases}\n"
"0, & \\Delta_k \\cdot \\Delta_{k-1} \\leq 0, \\\\\n"
"G_k, & \\text{otherwise.}\n"
"\\end{cases}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10093
msgid "**Computing the coefficients of the interpolation function**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10095
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Once $f_i'$ and $f_{i+1}'$ are known, the coefficients of the "
"interpolation function are"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10097
msgid "Constant term:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10099
msgid ""
"\n"
"d_i = y_i\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10103
msgid "Linear term:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10105
msgid ""
"\n"
"c_i = f_i'\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10109
msgid "Quadratic term:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10111
msgid ""
"\n"
"b_i = \\frac{3\\Delta_i - 2f_i' - f_{i+1}'}{\\Delta x_i}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10115
msgid "Cubic term:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:10117
msgid ""
"\n"
"a_i = \\frac{f_i' + f_{i+1}' - 2\\Delta_i}{\\Delta x_i \\Delta x_i}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20002
msgid "Monotonic Smoothing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20004
msgid ""
"Consider data $(x, y)$ sampled along a single ray, where $x$ increases "
"monotonically, while $y$ may show local rises because of noise, discrete "
"sampling, or local substructure. As a result, the raw data generally do "
"not satisfy global monotonicity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20006
msgid ""
"In `Gal3D`, we want to construct a smoothed function that stays as close "
"as possible to the original data while remaining globally monotonic, so "
"that it can later be inverted stably, that is, so that we can recover the"
" corresponding radius from a given function value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20008
msgid "Let the original sampled points be"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20010
msgid ""
"\n"
"x_1 < x_2 < \\cdots < x_n, \\qquad y_i = y(x_i).\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20014
msgid ""
"Below, we use the case of an overall monotonically decreasing target "
"function as an example. If the target is monotonically increasing, the "
"same procedure can be applied equivalently after reversing the sequence."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20016
msgid "1. Construct monotonic lower and upper envelopes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20018
msgid ""
"We first extract from the raw data the node sets of the monotonic lower "
"and upper envelopes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20020
msgid ""
"\n"
"\\mathcal{L} = \\{\\, j \\mid y_j = \\min_{k \\le j} y_k \\,\\},\n"
"\\qquad\n"
"\\mathcal{U} = \\{\\, j \\mid y_j = \\max_{k \\ge j} y_k \\,\\}.\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20026
msgid "Here:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20028
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"$\\mathcal{L}$ gives the cumulative minima obtained by scanning from the "
"inner side outward, and therefore defines the monotonic lower envelope."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20029
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"$\\mathcal{U}$ gives the cumulative maxima obtained by scanning from the "
"outer side inward, and therefore defines the monotonic upper envelope."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20031
msgid ""
"Applying PCHIP interpolation separately to these two node sets gives two "
"monotonic envelope functions,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20033
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"\n"
"y_{\\rm lower}(x), \\qquad y_{\\rm upper}(x).\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20037
msgid ""
"The advantage of PCHIP is that it preserves monotonicity, retains local "
"shape information as much as possible, and avoids the overshooting often "
"seen in ordinary cubic splines."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20039
msgid "2. Combine the upper and lower envelopes using local statistics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20041
msgid "If we simply take"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20043
msgid ""
"\n"
"\\frac{y_{\\rm upper}(x) + y_{\\rm lower}(x)}{2},\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20047
msgid ""
"we do obtain a curve that lies between the two envelopes, but it cannot "
"reflect whether the original data in each local interval are closer to "
"the upper envelope or to the lower envelope. Therefore, `Gal3D` combines "
"the two envelopes using a piecewise weighted scheme."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20049
msgid ""
"We first take the shared nodes of the upper and lower envelopes as "
"interval boundaries, dividing the whole domain into several intervals "
"$I_m$. In each interval, let $N_m$ be the total number of original "
"sampled points in that interval, and let $N_{{\\rm above},m}$ be the "
"number of points above the midline, where the midline is defined as"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20051
msgid ""
"\n"
"y_{\\rm mid}(x) = \\frac{y_{\\rm upper}(x) + y_{\\rm lower}(x)}{2}.\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20055
msgid "We then define the weight of that interval as"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20057
msgid ""
"\n"
"w_m = \\frac{1 + N_{{\\rm above},m}}{2 + N_m}.\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20061
msgid "The smoothed function in that interval is therefore"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20063
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"\n"
"\\tilde y(x)\n"
"=\n"
"w_m \\, y_{\\rm upper}(x)\n"
"+\n"
"(1 - w_m) \\, y_{\\rm lower}(x),\n"
"\\qquad x \\in I_m.\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20072
msgid "As a result:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20074
msgid ""
"because $0 < w_m < 1$, $\\tilde y(x)$ always lies between the upper and "
"lower envelopes;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20075
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"because both $y_{\\rm lower}(x)$ and $y_{\\rm upper}(x)$ are themselves "
"monotonically decreasing, and $w_m$ is constant within each interval, "
"$\\tilde y(x)$ also remains monotonically decreasing within each "
"interval."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20077
msgid ""
"Intuitively, if the raw points in a given interval are closer to the "
"upper envelope, then $w_m$ becomes larger and the smoothed result moves "
"closer to the upper envelope; otherwise, it moves closer to the lower "
"envelope."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20079
msgid "3. Final interpolation function"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20081
msgid ""
"After obtaining the piecewise-defined function $\\tilde y(x)$, we apply "
"PCHIP interpolation once more to construct the final smooth monotonic "
"function"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20083
msgid ""
"\n"
"\\hat y(x).\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20087
msgid ""
"The resulting $\\hat y(x)$ simultaneously satisfies the following "
"properties:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20089
msgid "it is globally monotonic;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20090
msgid "it is continuous and sufficiently smooth;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20091
msgid "it preserves the main local trends of the original data;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20092
msgid ""
"it can be stably inverted to construct the inverse function $x(\\hat y)$,"
" which is used to recover the corresponding radius from a target density."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/details/monotonic_ray.ipynb:20094
msgid ""
"The figure below shows an example: the black points are the raw data, the"
" green curves are the upper and lower bounds, and the red curve is the "
"target monotonic smooth interpolation function."
msgstr ""

